ing into problems and in my  experience coaching researchers  so  when a mistake is comes to  the forefront saying something like what  can we do to resolve this problem what  can we learn from this or if it's let  you know if you're like really down  about it which  i think this is almost exactly something  i said within the first month of one of  the projects i  i launched recently because one of the  newcomers to the team into the center  made a mistake his face was just  so upset and i was like this is not how  we hoped this launches this recruitment  feature our recruitment process of the  project would go but we'll figure it out  and his whole face changed like okay  she's not angry but i was not happy  either so that's why i was like  not how we helped it go but we'll figure  it out  um then things for clarifying  expectations  be saying like let me let me repeat what  i think we're planning to do then we can  discuss or fill in any  and there's other examples there  the second list of communication  functions these are more relationship  focused  so  definitely the way you handle those task  focused communications on the previous  slide  influence and inform relationship  building but these are more expressly  about relationships expressing  appreciation i really appreciate your  work on this providing support and  encouragement what what helps you need  or you can do this i'm confident in you  and then just connecting with people  just getting to know people for who they  are what are the biggest and who are the  biggest influences on your career so far  and why  what do you like most about this project  or this work  and then  perspective taking is sort of like the  cognitive twin of emotional empathy  where empathy is like a more affective  you're feeling other people's feelings  and perspective taking is sort of  imagining  what  how another person sees a situation and  these are really  underlie a lot of communication conflict  resolution and interpersonal interaction  so i wanted to take a second to say  um a few words about  some of the questions you can be asking  yourself so one is one is to just test  your assumptions what assumptions am i  making here what evidence do i have for  my thoughts to believe  um  next is just imagining the other  person's view so how might this person  think or feel about this situation  problem or issue and then  the last one is to really self-reflect  on things like what am i really  concerned about here what do i really  care about  am i admitting the limits of my  knowledge here how could i be deceiving  myself  these are sort of those internal sort of  the thoughts and questions that you have  in your own mind as you're going through  these interpersonal dynamics  and now i'm reaching towards the um last  few slides  and here's a other tips i always give  early career researchers  so  you've been told to develop your network  i'm just here to say yes  do not underestimate the value and  importance of developing your network  early and often  cultivate multiple mentors  don't expect one or two people to be a  super mentor that can provide you with  all the mentoring you need and also  go to peers peers provide great  mentoring  learn to seek help and outside  perspective or expertise  know the resource the resources  available to your institutions you can  tap into them  this is so hard learn to say no  ask people you really respect for more  senior to you how they've done it um you  can get some good insights don't forget  to celebrate successes and small wins  along the way because our research is  is a lot of work it's hard there's a lot  of setbacks so  forget not forgetting to stop and  celebrate even if it's in a small way  and that also builds um team  relationships to say yeah guys we nailed  it okay now i'm on the next thing  attending distress  in my coaching experience with  researchers  when stress and anxiety get out of  control it's just really difficult to  show