ining program at  cold spring harbor labs and the first  thing we did was get in small groups so  you could have the groups get in this  group and we talked about stories that  had happened in our scientific career  that taught us things that  we'll never forget around interacting in  scientific teams some of our stories  were positive and some were like horror  stories  um and man we bonded it was like group  therapy  and we just became instantly close in  that cohort of about 30 people and then  we were in smaller teams of like four or  so so just being intentional about how  to get people talking early and quickly  um just to get to know each other as  people and in their experience in a  scientific environment and then maybe  doing like a short simple activity of  like  how are we gonna make this work how are  we going to make sure we hear  everybody's voices  who's here at the table  who do we have here  what's important  yeah what's important to keep the energy  going i mean you kind of mentioned you  know a lot of meetings  documenting things  um you know what else can one do to kind  of keep the energy going after because  usually when you start something there's  a lot of energy  and it's like maintaining that is always  such a challenge any recommendations yep  so i do agree i do think having some  kind of routine meeting schedule that  we're going to have and then just say  we're going to touch base every two  weeks or every month or every week  depends on your project and what your  goals are but having that scheduled and  then you can always say no we need we  need to you can always cancel it  but  i am of the mind if it's not on your  calendar it's not on your agenda it's  not going to happen and for at least the  way we operate in our center having  those meanings create sort of momentum  and accountability so  um you know no one really loves to have  like an extra meaning but if you really  care about a project and a team making  the time for that is